Scenario 1: Pointers to Class Members
You’re developing a vehicle management system with a class Vehicle that has several data members (for example, an integer speed and a float fuelLevel) and a member function displayStatus(). Parts of the system need to dynamically modify and display these attributes using pointers to class members.

(a) How would you declare a pointer to the speed data member and a pointer to the member function displayStatus()?
(b) Provide a code snippet that uses these pointers to set the speed of a Vehicle object and then calls displayStatus().
(c) What are some potential pitfalls when using pointers to class members, and how can they be mitigated? 

(a) Declaring Pointers to Class Members

In C++, pointers to class members have a special syntax.

A pointer to a data member is declared as:

int Vehicle::*ptrToSpeed;

A pointer to a member function is declared as:

void (Vehicle::*ptrToDisplay)();


(b) Code Snippet Using These Pointers

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Vehicle {
public:
    int speed;
    float fuelLevel;

    void displayStatus() {
        cout << "Speed: " << speed << " km/h, Fuel Level: " << fuelLevel << " liters" << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Vehicle car;

    // Pointer to the speed data member
    int Vehicle::*ptrToSpeed = &Vehicle::speed;

    // Pointer to the displayStatus member function
    void (Vehicle::*ptrToDisplay)() = &Vehicle::displayStatus;

    // Modify the speed via the pointer
    car.*ptrToSpeed = 80;  // Set speed to 80
    car.fuelLevel = 50.5;  // Directly setting fuel level for demonstration

    // Call the display function via the pointer
    (car.*ptrToDisplay)();

    return 0;
}

(c) Potential Pitfalls and Mitigation

1. Syntax Complexity

Pointers to class members have an unusual syntax (object.*pointer or objectPtr->*pointer), which can be confusing.

Mitigation: Use typedef or using to make the syntax more readable.



2. Null Pointer Dereferencing

If an object pointer is nullptr, dereferencing the member pointer leads to undefined behavior.

Mitigation: Always check if the object is valid before accessing members.



3. Accessing Private Members

Pointers to members respect access control, so attempting to access a private member directly will cause a compilation error.

Mitigation: Use getters/setters for private members.



4. Polymorphism Issues



